Chapter: GopXhci
  cites Works chapter GopAhci
  cites Kernel chapter Pci
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 Post-ExitBootServices xHCI (USB 3.x) host controller driver. The
 stick the machine boots from is USB mass storage: the AHCI and IDE
 drivers reach only disks on those controllers, so persistence on the
 boot medium itself requires the payload to drive the USB host
 controller directly, with firmware gone.

 The controller is found by PCI class 0x0C subclass 0x03; BAR0 is the
 register block. Offsets inside it come from the capability header:
 CAPLENGTH locates the operational registers, DBOFF the doorbell
 array, RTSOFF the runtime registers. Bring-up follows the xHCI
 order: reset the controller, hand it scratchpad pages if it asks for
 them, program the device context base array and the command ring,
 hang an event ring off interrupter 0, then set Run. Commands --
 enable slot, address device, configure endpoint -- are TRBs on the
 command ring; their completions arrive as events. The driver polls
 the event ring by cycle bit, fuel-bounded: the Option A boot path
 has no IDT, so nothing here may rely on an interrupt or hang on a
 register that never changes.

 Rings are the real discipline, not a shortcut: every producer ring
 (command, endpoint zero, each bulk endpoint) carries its enqueue
 index and producer cycle state in a small header before the TRB
 array, ends in a pre-written link TRB with the toggle-cycle flag,
 and wraps by activating that link and flipping its cycle. A real
 controller caches ring position from the moment Address Device or
 Configure Endpoint reads it out of the input context -- rewinding a
 dequeue pointer behind its back is not a thing hardware permits, so
 the driver never does it. Consumed events are acknowledged by
 writing ERDP back, which real controllers require to know the ring
 is not full.

 We say:

Section: Capability and Operational Registers

  xhci-pci-class : Integer = 12
  xhci-pci-subclass : Integer = 3

  xh-cap-hcsparams1 : Integer = 4
  xh-cap-hcsparams2 : Integer = 8
  xh-cap-hccparams1 : Integer = 16
  xh-cap-dboff : Integer = 20
  xh-cap-rtsoff : Integer = 24

  xh-op-usbcmd : Integer = 0
  xh-op-usbsts : Integer = 4
  xh-op-crcr : Integer = 24
  xh-op-dcbaap : Integer = 48
  xh-op-config : Integer = 56
  xh-op-ports : Integer = 1024

  xh-cmd-run : Integer = 1
  xh-cmd-hcrst : Integer = 2
  xh-sts-hch : Integer = 1
  xh-sts-cnr : Integer = 2048

  xh-ir0-erstsz : Integer = 40
  xh-ir0-erstba : Integer = 48
  xh-ir0-erstba-hi : Integer = 52
  xh-ir0-erdp : Integer = 56
  xh-ir0-erdp-hi : Integer = 60

  xh-portsc-ccs : Integer = 1
  xh-portsc-ped : Integer = 2
  xh-portsc-pr : Integer = 16
  xh-portsc-pp : Integer = 512
  xh-portsc-csc : Integer = 131072
  xh-portsc-prc : Integer = 2097152

  xhci-fuel : Integer = 1000000

 HCCPARAMS1 states two things this driver must honour on real
 silicon: the context size (CSZ, bit 2 -- 64-byte contexts on most
 Intel parts, 32 on QEMU) and where the extended capability list
 begins (xECP, bits 31:16, a dword offset from the register base).
 The extended list is where USB Legacy Support lives: on a board
 whose firmware ran a legacy-USB SMM handler, the controller is
 still BIOS-owned when we inherit it, and a reset issued under that
 ownership either misfires or fights the handler. The handoff sets
 the OS-owned bit, waits for the BIOS-owned bit to clear, then
 silences every legacy SMI.

  xh-hcc-csz-shift : Integer = 2
  xh-hcc-ppc-shift : Integer = 3

  xhci-xecp-legsup : Integer = 1
  xhci-xecp-protocol : Integer = 2
  xhci-legsup-bios : Integer = #10000
  xhci-legsup-os : Integer = #1000000
  xhci-legctl-keep : Integer = #E1FEE
  xhci-legctl-smi-clear : Integer = #E0000000

 Real ports take time QEMU does not. A power-controlled controller
 (HCCPARAMS1.PPC) boots with its ports unpowered, so a connect can
 only appear after Port Power is set and a power-good interval
 elapses; a freshly powered port then debounces its connect for
 about a tenth of a second before it is trustworthy; and a completed
 port reset owes the device a reset-recovery interval before it is
 addressed. These are wall-clock waits, so they ride the PIT tick
 counter (cell 28672, ~18 Hz) that runtime-init started, with a
 fuel-bounded busy fallback for the case where the tick never
 advances -- garbage timing must never hang the boot.

  xhci-tick-cell : Integer = 28672
  xhci-settle-ticks : Integer = 3
  xhci-recovery-ticks : Integer = 1
  xhci-settle-fuel : Integer = 50000000

 On an Intel PCH of the era this arc targets, the USB2 root ports
 are shared between the companion EHCI controller and the xHCI, and
 firmware may leave them routed to EHCI -- in which case the xHCI
 honestly sees no device on those pins. Four PCI-config registers on
 the xHCI function switch them: the routing masks state which ports
 CAN move, and writing a mask into its routing register moves every
 movable port. The masks are read first and only a plausible mask
 (some ports, not the all-ones of unimplemented reserved space) is
 acted on, so a modern Intel part whose USB2 is natively xHCI --
 mask zero -- is left untouched, as is every non-Intel controller.

  xhci-pci-intel : Integer = #8086
  xhci-intel-xusb2pr : Integer = #D0
  xhci-intel-xusb2prm : Integer = #D4
  xhci-intel-usb3pssen : Integer = #D8
  xhci-intel-usb3prm : Integer = #DC

Section: TRB Vocabulary

 A TRB is four little-endian dwords: parameter low, parameter high,
 status, control. The control dword carries the TRB type in bits
 15:10 and the cycle bit in bit 0; a TRB belongs to the controller
 exactly when its cycle bit matches the ring's producer cycle state.

  trb-t-normal : Integer = 1
  trb-t-setup : Integer = 2
  trb-t-data : Integer = 3
  trb-t-status : Integer = 4
  trb-t-isoch : Integer = 5
  trb-t-link : Integer = 6
  trb-t-enable-slot : Integer = 9
  trb-t-address-dev : Integer = 11
  trb-t-config-ep : Integer = 12
  trb-t-reset-ep : Integer = 14
  trb-t-stop-ep : Integer = 15
  trb-t-set-deq : Integer = 16
  trb-t-xfer-event : Integer = 32
  trb-t-cmd-complete : Integer = 33

  trb-flag-ioc : Integer = 32
  trb-flag-idt : Integer = 64

  trb-cc-success : Integer = 1
  trb-cc-short : Integer = 13
  trb-cc-stall : Integer = 6
  trb-cc-usb-transaction-error : Integer = 4

Section: Producer Rings

 A ring handle is one allocation: a 64-byte header (enqueue index at
 offset 0, producer cycle state at offset 4) followed by sixty
 16-byte TRB slots. Slot 59 holds the link TRB, written at creation
 with the toggle-cycle flag and an invalid cycle bit; when the
 producer reaches it, activating it with the current cycle hands the
 whole lap to the controller and the producer wraps with its cycle
 flipped. Fifty-nine usable slots is far beyond any single command or
 BOT round this driver enqueues.

  xhci-ring-slots : Integer = 59
  xhci-ring-link-ctl : Integer = 6146

  xhci-ring-trbs : Integer -> Integer
  xhci-ring-trbs (ring) = ring + 64

  xhci-ring-new : Integer
  xhci-ring-new =
    let ring = alloc-zeroed 1024 64
    in let c = poke-32 ring 4 1
    in let l0 = poke-32 (ring + 64) 944 (ring + 64)
    in let l1 = poke-32 (ring + 64) 956 xhci-ring-link-ctl
    in ring

  xhci-ring-push : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-ring-push (ring) (plo) (phi) (sts) (ctl) =
    let enq = peek-32 ring 0
    in let cyc = peek-32 ring 4
    in if enq >= xhci-ring-slots then
      let l = poke-32 (ring + 64) 956 (bit-or xhci-ring-link-ctl cyc)
      in let c = poke-32 ring 4 (1 - cyc)
      in let e = poke-32 ring 0 0
      in xhci-ring-push ring plo phi sts ctl
    else let off = enq * 16
    in let w0 = poke-32 (ring + 64) off plo
    in let w1 = poke-32 (ring + 64) (off + 4) phi
    in let w2 = poke-32 (ring + 64) (off + 8) sts
    in let w3 = poke-32 (ring + 64) (off + 12) (bit-or ctl cyc)
    in poke-32 ring 0 (enq + 1)

 A halted endpoint is restarted by pointing the controller back at the
 ring, and the only position it may be given is one the producer has not
 yet filled: the current enqueue slot, carrying the producer's own cycle
 state as the dequeue cycle. Anything behind that is a TRB the controller
 has already consumed, and anything ahead of it is not written yet.

  xhci-ring-enq-addr : Integer -> Integer
  xhci-ring-enq-addr (ring) = ring + 64 + (peek-32 ring 0) * 16

  xhci-ring-cycle : Integer -> Integer
  xhci-ring-cycle (ring) = peek-32 ring 4

Section: Host State

 The host record holds register bases and ring addresses fixed at
 init. The event-ring dequeue index, its consumer cycle state, and
 the BOT tag counter live in a small state block in memory -- DMA
 rings are shared mutable state, and pretending otherwise just moves
 the mutation somewhere less visible.

  xh-st-evt-deq : Integer = 0
  xh-st-evt-cyc : Integer = 4
  xh-st-tag : Integer = 8

 The Endpoint ID of a Transfer Event is in dword 3 bits 16 to 20
 (xHCI 6.4.2.1); the Slot ID is bits 24 to 31 of the same dword.

  xh-st-latch : Integer = 64

  xhci-evt-count : Integer = 64

  XhciHost = record {
    xh-ok : Boolean,
    xh-op : Integer,
    xh-db : Integer,
    xh-rt : Integer,
    xh-ports : Integer,
    xh-dcbaa : Integer,
    xh-cmd-ring : Integer,
    xh-evt-ring : Integer,
    xh-state : Integer,
    xh-ctxsz : Integer,
    xh-cap : Integer,
    xh-xecp : Integer
  }

  xhci-no-host : XhciHost
  xhci-no-host = XhciHost { xh-ok = False, xh-op = 0, xh-db = 0, xh-rt = 0, xh-ports = 0, xh-dcbaa = 0, xh-cmd-ring = 0, xh-evt-ring = 0, xh-state = 0, xh-ctxsz = 32, xh-cap = 0, xh-xecp = 0 }

  UsbDev = record {
    ud-ok : Boolean,
    ud-slot : Integer,
    ud-port : Integer,
    ud-speed : Integer,
    ud-ctx : Integer,
    ud-ep0-ring : Integer,
    ud-route : Integer,
    ud-tt-slot : Integer,
    ud-tt-port : Integer
  }

 The route string is what says WHERE a device is. Without it a device
 addressed through two tiers of hub is indistinguishable from one on a
 root port, because every other field is the same -- the root port it
 descends from, its speed, its slot. A hub walk that cannot report
 where it arrived cannot be checked by anything.

 The transaction translator is carried on the device for the same
 reason. A full-speed device reaches a high-speed bus only through
 some ancestor hub's translator, and that ancestor is not always the
 hub it is plugged into -- a full-speed hub has none of its own and
 passes its parent's along. Recording which translator serves this
 device is what lets its children inherit the right one.

  xhci-no-dev : UsbDev
  xhci-no-dev = UsbDev { ud-ok = False, ud-slot = 0, ud-port = 0, ud-speed = 0, ud-ctx = 0, ud-ep0-ring = 0, ud-route = 0, ud-tt-slot = 0, ud-tt-port = 0 }

Section: Diagnostics

 On real metal there is no serial log, so the bring-up records what
 it found -- controller identity, capability parameters, the
 ownership handoff, and a raw snapshot of every root port the
 instant the controller starts -- into a fixed cell block that a
 later screen reads. The magic dword is written LAST so a partial
 capture (a bring-up that faulted midway) is never mistaken for a
 complete one; the field that never got written names how far the
 bring-up got.

 The block is otherwise observational, with one exception that must
 be stated rather than discovered: the handback below READS it. Index
 19 holds the operational base and index 39 the firmware's own
 pre-handoff control word, which together are everything needed to
 give the controller back -- so a caller that holds no XhciHost (the
 first-boot ceremony, which waits for a key long after the bring-up
 returned) can still return the machine to firmware. Indices 28 and
 29 record that decision. Occupied: 0-19 identity and bring-up,
 20-27 the per-port snapshot, 28-29 the handback, 30-32 the BAR window
 verdict and the address it judged, 36-39 the Intel routing and the
 saved control word.

 Index 30 is 1 for used-as-written and 2 for relocated, never 0, so a
 zero distinguishes "the judgement was never reached" from either
 verdict. A two-valued cell cannot make that distinction, and on a
 board where the firmware places the controller at xhci-reloc-base the
 two branches are otherwise indistinguishable from the outside.

 31 and 32 are the LOW and HIGH dwords of the address judged, and both
 are needed. A BAR above 4 GB carries no address bits at all in its low
 dword -- the ASUS answers 00000004 there, all of the address being the
 c0 in the high one -- so a reading that shows only the low dword shows
 a zero and invites the conclusion that the BAR was never read.

 Indices 0 to 39 describe ONE controller and are overwritten by the next
 bring-up, so after a full walk they describe the controller the keyboard
 was found on, or the last one brought up when no keyboard was found. 40
 upwards are the per-machine cells. 40 is how many xHCI
 functions the whole bus tree holds, 41 how many were brought up, 42 a
 bitmask of which ordinals were walked. 43 and 44 name the controller the
 disk and the keyboard came from as ordinal PLUS ONE, so zero means no
 controller yielded one rather than controller zero did.

 48 upwards is four dwords per controller for the first four: identity,
 location, outcome, and what was found there. The outcome is three-valued
 for the reason index 30 is -- 0 "never reached" must not read the same as
 1 "opened, bring-up failed" or 2 "running and walked".

 64-69 are the boot keyboard's interrupt endpoint, and they exist to be a
 COMPARISON: what the device's descriptor asked for beside what we
 programmed into its endpoint context. A value alone is satisfied by any
 plausible number, so a mismatch is the finding and a lone reading is not.
 64 is three-valued for the same reason 30 and the outcome above are: a
 bInterval of zero because the descriptor was never fetched must not read
 the same as a bInterval genuinely zero.

 70-74 are the mass-storage bring-up ladder, owned by GopUsbMsc: the
 furthest rung reached, the Configure Endpoint completion code, the block
 size the medium reported, the sector count, and the bConfigurationValue
 the driver sent. They are three-valued in the same way -- a zero rung is
 a bring-up that never reached the first one, and there is no completion
 code zero for the second cell to collide with.

 74 is written BEFORE the request it describes is issued, which is the
 point of it: a refused SET_CONFIGURATION has to be able to show the value
 that was refused, or the reading cannot tell a wrong value from a device
 that refuses every value.

 75-78 say WHERE the mass-storage device was and what actually came back
 from its SET_CONFIGURATION: controller ordinal plus one, port, speed and
 slot packed a byte each, then the route string, then the completion code,
 then that port's own PORTSC. The code is the point -- a STALL, a
 Transaction Error and the fuel expiring are three different faults and the
 Boolean rendered them as one. The PORTSC is taken AT THE DEVICE rather
 than left to the snapshot, which covers eight ports on a controller that
 may have twenty-six.

 46 is a bitmask of which root ports report a device connected, one bit per
 port up to 32. Index 18 counts them, and a count names none of them: the
 ASUS answered connected=4 with the device that mattered on port 9, which
 no snapshot row reaches.

 79 is the completion code of a SECOND SET_CONFIGURATION, asked only when
 the first was refused and never acted on. A transient failure and a
 permanent one want opposite fixes and one attempt cannot tell them apart;
 the cell turns "a retry might help" from a guess into a measurement, at
 the cost of one idempotent request.

 79 is the last index in use, so the block spans 320 bytes from its base.
 It was 69 and 280 until the ladder was added on 2026-08-03.

 The base is 0x1D000: the hole between the IST emergency stacks
 ([0x15000, 0x1D000), sixteen 2 KB stacks) and the AP idle stacks at
 0x20000. Nothing can grow into it without first overrunning the AP idle
 stacks, which is a bound the layout already defends.

 The hole has THREE tenants and this block no longer owns it to the top.
 fester's GOP handoff block took 0x1F000 (126976), 48 bytes, on
 2026-07-30, and KbdDiagProbe's `kx-cell` took 119808 to 119875 -- inside
 what this paragraph used to call free, placed deliberately a kilobyte
 past the 96 dwords `xdiag-zero` claims. So the map is: this block 118784
 to 119079, free to 119807, kx-cell to 119875, free to 126975, the
 handoff block, then 4048 free bytes below the AP idle stacks.

 Growth here stops at 119808, not at 0x1F000 and not at 0x20000, and the
 96-dword wipe is the real ceiling for anything that must be zeroed on
 metal: a cell above index 95 is never cleared and reads firmware
 residue.

 It was at 36200 until 2026-07-30, inside the PML4 page, where indices
 0-15 aliased eight X86_64Boot runtime cells at 36200-36263: a bring-up
 wrote the PCI vendor id over uefi-systab, raised fs-elevated, and
 disturbed ap-id-next. 36200 was picked by grepping tools/codex-vm.c,
 which answers half the question -- the host does claim 36152, 36160 and
 36168 in that band, but this collision was with the GUEST. Grep both
 sides before claiming a fixed cell.

  xhci-diag : Integer = 118784
  xhci-diag-magic : Integer = #58444731

 The ordinal of the controller currently being walked, plus one. Indices 0
 to 39 are restored from the snapshot taken when the keyboard was credited,
 so on a two-controller machine they describe the KEYBOARD's controller and
 a device found on the other one has nothing on screen naming where it was.
 45 is written as each controller's walk begins, so anything recorded
 during that walk can say which one it happened on.

  xhci-cur-ord : Integer = 45

  xhci-ctl-max : Integer = 4
  xhci-ctl-base : Integer = 48
  xhci-ctl-failed : Integer = 1
  xhci-ctl-running : Integer = 2

  xhci-ep-base : Integer = 64
  xhci-ep-failed : Integer = 1
  xhci-ep-ok : Integer = 2

  xhci-ep-status : XhciEvent -> Integer
  xhci-ep-status (ce) =
    if ce.xe-ok == False then xhci-ep-failed
    else if ce.xe-code /= trb-cc-success then xhci-ep-failed
    else xhci-ep-ok

 The status goes in LAST, so a bring-up that faults midway leaves it zero
 and the reading says the fields were never taken rather than showing the
 residue of whatever the firmware left.

  xhci-ep-note : UsbDev, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-ep-note (dev) (epnum) (maxpkt) (binterval) (interval) (status) =
    let a = xdiag-put (xhci-ep-base + 1) (bit-or (bit-and binterval 65535) (bit-shl (bit-and maxpkt 65535) 16))
    in let b = xdiag-put (xhci-ep-base + 2) (bit-or (bit-and interval 65535) (bit-shl (bit-and maxpkt 65535) 16))
    in let c = xdiag-put (xhci-ep-base + 3) (dev.ud-route)
    in let d = xdiag-put (xhci-ep-base + 4) (bit-or (bit-and (dev.ud-tt-slot) 65535) (bit-shl (bit-and (dev.ud-tt-port) 65535) 16))
    in let e = xdiag-put (xhci-ep-base + 5) (bit-or (bit-and (dev.ud-speed) 255) (bit-or (bit-shl (bit-and (epnum * 2 + 1) 255) 8) (bit-shl (bit-and epnum 255) 16)))
    in xdiag-put xhci-ep-base status

  xdiag-put : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xdiag-put (i) (v) = poke-32 xhci-diag (i * 4) v

  xdiag-get : Integer -> Integer
  xdiag-get (i) = peek-32 xhci-diag (i * 4)

 Metal RAM is not zeroed. A cell whose feature never ran holds whatever
 the firmware left at this address, and on the ASUS that read as
 disk-ctl=2760958976 walked=7f BAR=672 -- garbage wearing the costume of
 a measurement. Every emulator bed zero-fills guest RAM, so a zero there
 reads as an honest "never written" and the difference was invisible
 until a real board rendered its leftovers.

  xdiag-zero : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xdiag-zero (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then 0
    else let w = poke-32 xhci-diag (i * 4) 0
    in xdiag-zero (i + 1) n

 Which controller 0-39 describe is not a presentation question. The
 handback in KbdDiagProbe computes the capability base as the KEYBOARD
 host's operational base minus cell 4, then scans from there for the
 legacy-support capability using cell 8 and writes cell 39 back into it.
 On a machine with one controller those agree by accident. On a machine
 with two they name different controllers, and the handback then walks a
 capability list at an address derived from mismatched halves.

 So the walk keeps a copy of 0-39 taken at the moment the keyboard is
 credited and puts it back once every controller is up. A copy is exact
 where a re-read is not: cell 39 is the firmware's pre-handoff control
 word and 20-27 are the port snapshot taken the instant the controller
 started, and neither exists to be read a second time.

  xdiag-span : Integer = 40

  xdiag-save : Integer -> Integer
  xdiag-save (buf) = xdiag-save-at buf 0

  xdiag-save-at : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xdiag-save-at (buf) (i) =
    if i >= xdiag-span then 0
    else let v = poke-32 buf (i * 4) (xdiag-get i)
    in xdiag-save-at buf (i + 1)

  xdiag-load : Integer -> Integer
  xdiag-load (buf) = xdiag-load-at buf 0

  xdiag-load-at : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xdiag-load-at (buf) (i) =
    if i >= xdiag-span then 0
    else let v = xdiag-put i (peek-32 buf (i * 4))
    in xdiag-load-at buf (i + 1)

Section: MMIO Hole Mapping

 The controller's registers live in the PCI MMIO hole above 3 GB. The
 standard boot path maps exactly bare-metal-ram-size, so the fourth
 gigabyte has no page-directory entry and the first register read
 page-faults. If the PDPT's fourth entry is empty, install one: a
 fresh page directory identity-mapping [3 GB, 4 GB) as 2 MB pages,
 uncached (PCD) and non-executable -- device registers, not memory.
 Under the Option A stub the live tables already map 4 GB and are not
 at the runtime-table address anyway; there the entry reads non-empty
 or the write lands in dead memory, harmless either way.

  xhci-pdpt : Integer = 36864
  xhci-pde-flags : Integer = #93

  xhci-map-mmio-hole : Integer
  xhci-map-mmio-hole =
    if peek-32 xhci-pdpt 24 /= 0 then 1
    else let pd = alloc-zeroed 4096 4096
    in let f = xhci-fill-pd pd 0
    in let lo = poke-32 xhci-pdpt 24 (pd + 3)
    in poke-32 xhci-pdpt 28 0

  xhci-fill-pd : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-fill-pd (pd) (i) =
    if i >= 512 then 0
    else let lo = poke-32 pd (i * 8) (#C0000000 + i * #200000 + xhci-pde-flags)
    in let hi = poke-32 pd (i * 8 + 4) #80000000
    in xhci-fill-pd pd (i + 1)

Section: Controller Bring-Up

  xhci-wait-bit-clear : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-wait-bit-clear (base) (reg) (mask) (fuel) =
    if bit-and (peek-32 base reg) mask == 0 then 1
    else if fuel <= 0 then 0
    else xhci-wait-bit-clear base reg mask (fuel - 1)

  xhci-wait-bit-set : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-wait-bit-set (base) (reg) (mask) (fuel) =
    if bit-and (peek-32 base reg) mask /= 0 then 1
    else if fuel <= 0 then 0
    else xhci-wait-bit-set base reg mask (fuel - 1)

 A controller must be halted before it is reset: resetting a running
 controller is undefined. Clear Run/Stop and wait for HCHalted; a
 controller firmware already stopped answers at once.

  xhci-halt : Integer -> Integer
  xhci-halt (op) =
    let cur = peek-32 op xh-op-usbcmd
    in if bit-and cur xh-cmd-run == 0 then xhci-wait-bit-set op xh-op-usbsts xh-sts-hch xhci-fuel
    else let w = poke-32 op xh-op-usbcmd (bit-and cur (-2))
    in xhci-wait-bit-set op xh-op-usbsts xh-sts-hch xhci-fuel

 The extended capability list is a linked chain of dwords: each
 carries its capability id in bits 7:0 and the dword offset to the
 next in bits 15:8, zero ending the chain. The offset accumulates
 from the register base, so each hop adds the pointer to the current
 offset. Legacy Support is capability id 1.

  xhci-find-legsup : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-find-legsup (base) (offdw) (fuel) =
    if offdw == 0 then 0
    else if fuel <= 0 then 0
    else let addr = base + offdw * 4
    in let cap = peek-32 addr 0
    in let nxt = bit-and (bit-shru cap 8) 255
    in if bit-and cap 255 == xhci-xecp-legsup then addr
    else if nxt == 0 then 0
    else xhci-find-legsup base (offdw + nxt) (fuel - 1)

 Capability id 2 is Supported Protocol, and it is what says whether the
 PORTSC Port Speed field may be read as a number we already know. Dword
 two carries Compatible Port Offset in bits 7:0, Compatible Port Count in
 15:8 and Protocol Speed ID Count (PSIC) in 31:28. Each of the PSIC
 dwords that follow dword three carries PSIV in bits 3:0, PSIE in 5:4 and
 the mantissa PSIM in 31:16, the bit rate being PSIM scaled by PSIE as
 bits, kilobits, megabits or gigabits per second (xHCI 1.2c, Table 7-8,
 Table 7-10).

 Table 7-13's speed ids are the DEFAULT mapping and footnote 120 states
 the condition on them: "The Default Speed ID Values shall be presented
 in PORTSC Port Speed field only if no PSI Dwords are defined
 (PSIC = '0')." Table 7-8 says the same from the other side: a non-zero
 PSIC means "no implied Speed ID mappings apply". So a controller that
 declares its own PSI dwords may report any PSIV it likes for a
 Full-speed port, and reading that value as Table 7-13's 1 is a guess.

  xhci-protocol-covers : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  xhci-protocol-covers (addr) (port) =
    let d2 = peek-32 addr 8
    in let first = bit-and d2 255
    in let count = bit-and (bit-shru d2 8) 255
    in if port < first then False
    else port < first + count

  xhci-find-protocol : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-find-protocol (base) (offdw) (port) (fuel) =
    if offdw == 0 then 0
    else if fuel <= 0 then 0
    else let addr = base + offdw * 4
    in let cap = peek-32 addr 0
    in let nxt = bit-and (bit-shru cap 8) 255
    in if bit-and cap 255 /= xhci-xecp-protocol then xhci-find-protocol-next base offdw nxt port fuel
    else if xhci-protocol-covers addr port then addr
    else xhci-find-protocol-next base offdw nxt port fuel

  xhci-find-protocol-next : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-find-protocol-next (base) (offdw) (nxt) (port) (fuel) =
    if nxt == 0 then 0
    else xhci-find-protocol base (offdw + nxt) port (fuel - 1)

  xhci-psi-scale : Integer -> Integer
  xhci-psi-scale (e) =
    if e == 0 then 1
    else if e == 1 then 1000
    else if e == 2 then 1000000
    else 1000000000

 The thresholds separate the four rates Table 7-13 defines, at gaps wide
 enough that a controller rounding its mantissa (7.2.1 permits it, "the
 PSIM should be rounded to the closest value") cannot cross one: 1.5 Mb/s
 low, 12 Mb/s full, 480 Mb/s high, 5 Gb/s and above SuperSpeed.

  xhci-rate-speed : Integer -> Integer
  xhci-rate-speed (rate) =
    if rate < 3000000 then 2
    else if rate < 100000000 then 1
    else if rate < 1000000000 then 3
    else 4

  xhci-psi-lookup : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-psi-lookup (addr) (n) (raw) (i) =
    if i >= n then 0
    else let psi = peek-32 addr (i * 4)
    in if bit-and psi 15 /= raw then xhci-psi-lookup addr n raw (i + 1)
    else xhci-rate-speed ((bit-and (bit-shru psi 16) 65535) * xhci-psi-scale (bit-and (bit-shru psi 4) 3))

 A port with no Supported Protocol capability covering it, or one whose
 capability declares PSIC zero, is the case footnote 120 permits and the
 raw value stands. A declared PSIV that no PSI dword defines is the
 controller contradicting itself, and the answer is zero -- the same
 answer a port with nothing attached gives, which every caller already
 treats as "do not open this". Guessing a speed there would put a
 Full-speed device on the high-speed paths silently.

  xhci-speed-resolve : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-speed-resolve (base) (xecp) (port) (raw) =
    if xecp == 0 then raw
    else if raw == 0 then 0
    else let cap = xhci-find-protocol base xecp (port + 1) 256
    in if cap == 0 then raw
    else let psic = bit-and (bit-shru (peek-32 cap 8) 28) 15
    in if psic == 0 then raw
    else xhci-psi-lookup (cap + 16) psic raw 0

 The resolved class is for OUR decisions. The Slot Context Speed field is
 not one of them: Table 6-4 bits 23:20 says of it "Refer to the PORTSC Port
 Speed field in Table 5-27 for the definition of the valid values", and
 Table 5-27 defines PORTSC values 1 to 15 as "Protocol Speed ID (PSI)".
 So that field speaks the CONTROLLER's vocabulary, not ours, and on a
 controller declaring PSI dwords the class and the id are different
 numbers. The field is live rather than vestigial: 4.10.2.8 forbids CErr
 of zero "when the Slot Context Speed field indicates a Full- or Low-speed
 device", and 4.23.5.2 reads it for PING scheduling.

 A device behind a hub gets its speed from the hub's port status, which
 has no PSI value in it at all, so the encode is a reverse lookup by rate
 rather than a pass-through of what PORTSC said. Where no declared PSI
 dword carries the class, the class itself is written: that is a
 controller whose vocabulary cannot express the device, and it is what
 this driver did before it read PSI dwords at all, so it is a guess that
 is no worse rather than a new refusal.

  xhci-psi-encode : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-psi-encode (addr) (n) (cls) (i) =
    if i >= n then 0
    else let psi = peek-32 addr (i * 4)
    in let rate = (bit-and (bit-shru psi 16) 65535) * xhci-psi-scale (bit-and (bit-shru psi 4) 3)
    in if xhci-rate-speed rate /= cls then xhci-psi-encode addr n cls (i + 1)
    else bit-and psi 15

  xhci-speed-encode : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-speed-encode (base) (xecp) (port) (cls) =
    if xecp == 0 then cls
    else if cls == 0 then 0
    else let cap = xhci-find-protocol base xecp (port + 1) 256
    in if cap == 0 then cls
    else let psic = bit-and (bit-shru (peek-32 cap 8) 28) 15
    in if psic == 0 then cls
    else let v = xhci-psi-encode (cap + 16) psic cls 0
    in if v == 0 then cls
    else v

  xhci-wait-legsup : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-wait-legsup (addr) (fuel) =
    if bit-and (peek-32 addr 0) xhci-legsup-bios == 0 then 1
    else if fuel <= 0 then 0
    else xhci-wait-legsup addr (fuel - 1)

 Take the controller from firmware before touching it. With no
 legacy-support capability there is nothing to claim -- the
 controller is already ours. Otherwise set the OS-owned bit, wait
 for firmware to drop BIOS-owned, then write the legacy control/
 status register to keep only the non-SMI bits and clear every SMI
 event. The handoff's before/after words are recorded so a board
 that refuses to release shows exactly that, and the pre-handoff
 control/status word is recorded (diag index 39) so a handback
 experiment can restore the firmware's own SMI enables verbatim.

 Route the Intel USB2 ports to xHCI. Non-Intel controllers and
 implausible masks (nothing routable, or the all-ones of reserved
 space) are left alone. Enable SuperSpeed on the SS-routable ports,
 then route the USB2-routable ports; the masks and the applied flag
 are recorded for the truth screen. Pure PCI-config access, before
 the controller is reset.

  xhci-mask-implausible : Integer -> Boolean
  xhci-mask-implausible (m) = m == 0 | m == #FFFFFFFF

  xhci-intel-route : PciDevice -> Integer
  xhci-intel-route (d) =
    if d.pci-vendor /= xhci-pci-intel then xdiag-put 36 0
    else let ss = pci-read-config (d.pci-bus) (d.pci-dev) (d.pci-func) xhci-intel-usb3prm
    in let u2 = pci-read-config (d.pci-bus) (d.pci-dev) (d.pci-func) xhci-intel-xusb2prm
    in if xhci-mask-implausible u2 then xdiag-put 36 0
    else let w0 = if xhci-mask-implausible ss then 0 else pci-write-config (d.pci-bus) (d.pci-dev) (d.pci-func) xhci-intel-usb3pssen ss
    in let w1 = pci-write-config (d.pci-bus) (d.pci-dev) (d.pci-func) xhci-intel-xusb2pr u2
    in let a = xdiag-put 37 u2
    in let b = xdiag-put 38 ss
    in xdiag-put 36 1

  xhci-take-ownership : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-take-ownership (base) (xecp) =
    if xecp == 0 then xdiag-put 9 0
    else let addr = xhci-find-legsup base xecp 256
    in if addr == 0 then xdiag-put 9 0
    else let f = xdiag-put 9 1
    in let before = peek-32 addr 0
    in let b0 = xdiag-put 10 before
    in let req = poke-32 addr 0 (bit-or before xhci-legsup-os)
    in let waited = xhci-wait-legsup addr xhci-fuel
    in let ctl = peek-32 addr 4
    in let c9 = xdiag-put 39 ctl
    in let dis = poke-32 addr 4 (bit-or (bit-and ctl xhci-legctl-keep) xhci-legctl-smi-clear)
    in let after = peek-32 addr 0
    in let a0 = xdiag-put 11 after
    in xdiag-put 12 (if bit-and after xhci-legsup-bios == 0 then 1 else 0)

Section: Ownership Handback

 The reverse of the handoff, and the reason it exists: on a board
 whose firmware presents its USB keyboard as a PS/2 one through an
 SMM legacy handler, taking ownership by the book DISABLES that
 emulation. If the pure USB path then fails to deliver a keystroke,
 the machine has no keyboard at all -- and that is exactly what
 happened here on real hardware: the working
 path was scuttled in the same change that introduced its
 replacement, and the replacement did not hold weight.

 So the cord goes back. Halt the controller, so firmware inherits a
 quiescent device rather than a running one; restore the SMI enables
 the firmware itself had chosen, verbatim, from the word saved at
 the handoff; clear the OS-owned semaphore; and watch for BIOS-owned
 to return. A firmware may take a measurable stretch of SMM work to
 re-own a controller, so the wait is fuel-bounded and its outcome is
 recorded rather than asserted -- a board that never re-owns says so
 at index 29 instead of hanging.

 What this costs, stated plainly: the controller is shared, so a USB
 mass-storage boot stick dies with the keyboard's endpoint. The
 caller must therefore only reach for this when there is no keyboard
 at all, which is a machine that cannot be driven anyway. A keyboard
 and no stick beats a stick and no keyboard.

  xhci-legsup-os-mask : Integer = #FEFFFFFF
  xhci-release-fuel : Integer = 200000

  xhci-reclaim-wait : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-reclaim-wait (addr) (fuel) =
    if bit-and (peek-32 addr 0) xhci-legsup-bios /= 0 then 1
    else if fuel <= 0 then 0
    else xhci-reclaim-wait addr (fuel - 1)

  xhci-released : Integer -> Integer
  xhci-released (d) = xdiag-get 28

  xhci-release-ownership : Integer -> Integer
  xhci-release-ownership (d) =
    if xdiag-get 9 == 0 then xdiag-put 28 1
    else let op = xdiag-get 19
    in if op == 0 then xdiag-put 28 1
    else let h = xhci-halt op
    in let base = op - xdiag-get 4
    in let addr = xhci-find-legsup base (xdiag-get 8) 256
    in if addr == 0 then xdiag-put 28 1
    else let rc = poke-32 addr 4 (xdiag-get 39)
    in let os = poke-32 addr 0 (bit-and (peek-32 addr 0) xhci-legsup-os-mask)
    in let w = xdiag-put 29 (xhci-reclaim-wait addr xhci-release-fuel)
    in xdiag-put 28 1

 A raw PORTSC snapshot of the root ports the moment the controller runs,
 plus a count of ports reporting a device connected. This is the single
 most telling line on a board that enumerates nothing: all-zero connect
 bits point at port power or chipset routing, live connect bits at the
 enumeration path above.

 THE SNAPSHOT OWNS EIGHT CELLS AND MUST NOT WRITE A NINTH. The band is
 20-27; 28-29 are the handback, 30-32 the BAR verdict. This loop stopped
 at port 16 and wrote `20 + port`, so on any controller with more than
 eight root ports it laid PORTSC over all of them. The ASUS reports
 ports=26 and photographed `BAR verdict=672`, which is not one of the
 three values a verdict can take: 672 is 0x2A0, port 10's PORTSC, sitting
 in cell 30.

 Cell 28 is the one that costs something. `kbd-pump` stops dead on
 `xhci-released /= 0`, and cell 28 was receiving port 8's PORTSC, whose
 Port Power bit alone makes it non-zero. The chapter above blamed that on
 firmware residue in unzeroed metal RAM; `xhci-reset-handback` already
 zeroes 28 and 29 on the way in, so residue was never the mechanism. It
 was this loop, and it wrote the cell AFTER the wipe.

 QEMU could not show it and the reason is not that it zeroes RAM. Its
 controller reports exactly EIGHT ports, so the write lands on the last
 legal cell and stops. The bed sat precisely on the boundary.

 The bound is the cell count, not a second literal, so the two cannot
 drift apart again. Ports at or above the bound are still COUNTED -- the
 connected total is the number the enumeration path is judged against and
 it must cover every port, which is how a device on port 9 shows up in
 `connected=` while having no PORTSC row of its own.

  xhci-port-cells : Integer = 8

 A COUNT of connected ports names none of them, and on a controller wider
 than the eight rows above it that is the difference between a reading and
 a rumour: the ASUS answered connected=4 while the device that mattered
 sat on port 9, which no row could show. One bit per port, up to 32, says
 WHICH -- and it is the only place a port above the snapshot band appears
 at all.

  xhci-conn-mask : Integer = 46

  xhci-diag-ports : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-diag-ports (op) (port) (maxports) (nconn) =
    if port >= maxports then xdiag-put 18 nconn
    else let raw = peek-32 op (xh-op-ports + port * 16)
    in let w = if port >= xhci-port-cells then 0 else xdiag-put (20 + port) raw
    in let conn = bit-and raw xh-portsc-ccs /= 0
    in let m = if conn & (port < 32) then xdiag-put xhci-conn-mask (bit-or (xdiag-get xhci-conn-mask) (bit-shl 1 port)) else 0
    in let c = if conn then nconn + 1 else nconn
    in xhci-diag-ports op (port + 1) maxports c

 Wait n PIT ticks, exiting the instant the counter has advanced far
 enough. If the tick never moves -- an environment where the timer
 interrupt is not delivered -- the fuel bounds a busy spin so timing
 alone can never hang the boot.

  xhci-delay-wait : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-delay-wait (start) (n) (fuel) =
    if peek-qword xhci-tick-cell 0 - start >= n then 1
    else if fuel <= 0 then 0
    else xhci-delay-wait start n (fuel - 1)

  xhci-delay-ticks : Integer -> Integer
  xhci-delay-ticks (n) =
    let start = peek-qword xhci-tick-cell 0
    in xhci-delay-wait start n xhci-settle-fuel

 Set Port Power on every root port. PORTSC carries write-1-to-clear
 change bits, so the write names only the intent -- Port Power alone
 -- and never echoes the read value back, the same discipline the
 reset path follows. Harmless on a controller that powers its ports
 unconditionally.

  xhci-power-ports : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-power-ports (op) (port) (maxports) =
    if port >= maxports then 0
    else let w = poke-32 op (xh-op-ports + port * 16) xh-portsc-pp
    in xhci-power-ports op (port + 1) maxports

  xhci-enable-device : PciDevice -> [Device.Port] Integer
  xhci-enable-device (d) =
    let cmd = pci-read-config (d.pci-bus) (d.pci-dev) (d.pci-func) 4
    in pci-write-config (d.pci-bus) (d.pci-dev) (d.pci-func) 4 (bit-or cmd 6)

 Every cell this block DRIVES from must be zeroed before it is read.
 The observational fields are safe by the magic-written-last rule --
 a partial capture is detected. The handback fields are not: they are
 STATE, they gate the USB pump, and a machine that comes up with cell 28
 already non-zero believes it has handed the controller back. It then
 prints that it has, and stops pumping the keyboard it never released.

 This zeroing is correct and stays. What it does NOT do is explain the
 ASUS, and this block used to claim it did. Cell 28 was being written
 after the wipe by `xhci-diag-ports`, which stopped at port 16 while the
 snapshot owns only 20-27, so port 8's PORTSC landed on the handback flag
 on any controller with more than eight ports. Fixed 2026-08-03 at that
 loop. The tell was in the first photograph of the board: `BAR verdict=672`,
 a value the verdict cannot hold, which is port 10's PORTSC in cell 30.

 The claim that QEMU "zeroes its RAM and could not fail this" was true of
 the wrong mechanism. Its controller reports exactly eight ports, so the
 overrun lands on the last legal cell. The bed sat on the boundary.

  xhci-reset-handback : Integer -> Integer
  xhci-reset-handback (d) =
    let a = xdiag-put 28 0
    in xdiag-put 29 0

  xhci-connect-none : XhciHost
  xhci-connect-none =
    let h = xhci-reset-handback 0
    in let a = xdiag-put 1 0
    in let m = xdiag-put 0 xhci-diag-magic
    in xhci-no-host

  xhci-ctl-put : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-ctl-put (ord) (slot) (v) =
    if ord >= xhci-ctl-max then 0
    else xdiag-put (xhci-ctl-base + ord * 4 + slot) v

 The identity and location are recorded BEFORE the bring-up, with the
 outcome pre-set to failed, so a bring-up that faults partway leaves a row
 naming the controller it died on instead of an empty one.

  xhci-ctl-note : PciDevice, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-ctl-note (d) (ord) =
    let i = xhci-ctl-put ord 0 (bit-or (bit-shl (d.pci-vendor) 16) (d.pci-device-id))
    in let l = xhci-ctl-put ord 1 (bit-or (bit-shl (d.pci-bus) 16) (bit-or (bit-shl (d.pci-dev) 8) (d.pci-func)))
    in xhci-ctl-put ord 2 xhci-ctl-failed

 The scan is taken once and handed in. `pci-scan-all` walks the bridge
 tree and allocates as it goes, and there is no collector, so a caller
 that re-scanned per controller would pay for the whole tree again on
 every round.

  xhci-connect-at : PciScanResult, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] XhciHost
  xhci-connect-at (scan) (from) (ord) = act
    idx <- pci-index-class scan xhci-pci-class xhci-pci-subclass from
    if idx < 0 then xhci-connect-none
    else xhci-connect-one (list-at (scan.devices) idx) ord
  end

  xhci-connect-one : PciDevice, Integer -> [Device.Port] XhciHost
  xhci-connect-one (d) (ord) = act
    n <- xhci-ctl-note d ord
    xh <- xhci-init-device d ord
    let s = xhci-ctl-put ord 2 (if xh.xh-ok then xhci-ctl-running else xhci-ctl-failed)
    in xh
  end

  xhci-connect : [Device.Port] XhciHost
  xhci-connect = act
    scan <- pci-scan-all
    let c = xdiag-put 40 (pci-count-class scan xhci-pci-class xhci-pci-subclass)
    in xhci-connect-at scan 0 0
  end

 Real firmware may place a 64-bit BAR above 4 GB (OVMF parks the xHCI
 at 0xC000000000), beyond the boot identity map. Post-EBS the machine
 is ours, so the driver relocates the BAR into the 32-bit MMIO hole:
 memory decoding off, write both BAR dwords, decoding back on, then
 trust nothing until the readback confirms the move. The address is a
 convention -- the top of the hole, clear of MMCONFIG and of
 firmware's ascending 32-bit assignments -- until a real allocator
 arrives with the ACPI work.

 The judgment "above 4 GB" must read BOTH dwords. A lone device gets
 the 4 GB-aligned base of the window and its low dword reads zero;
 add a second 64-bit device and firmware packs the window, parking
 this controller at an address like 0xC000004000 whose low dword is
 a perfectly plausible-looking 0x4000. Trusting a nonzero low dword
 alone brings the controller up against low RAM and every register
 read is garbage -- a keyboard that vanishes the moment an NVMe disk
 is plugged in.

 Above 4 GB is not the only unusable place a BAR can be, and it is the
 SAFE one: it is outside every page table the runtime installs, so the
 first register read faults and says so. A BAR BELOW 3 GB does not fault
 at all. The runtime maps 0 to 3 GB identity as RAM, which is the arena
 `alloc-bytes` hands out, so the controller's registers and the heap
 become the same bytes and the damage surfaces somewhere with no BAR in
 it. red measured OVMF placing a NIC at 0x81060000, 2.16 GB, squarely
 inside the arena, and the exposure belongs to any driver that reads a
 BAR rather than to that one (`e1000-bar-verdict`, `E1000e`).

 So the test is membership of the device window, not the height of one
 dword. A BAR is used where it lies only when it is memory, non-zero,
 32-bit-addressable and inside [3 GB, 4 GB); everything else relocates,
 which this driver can do and a NIC driver cannot. The verdict and the
 address it judged are recorded, because a board that lands somewhere
 unexpected should say so rather than be inferred from a dead screen.

  xhci-window-lo : Integer = 3221225472
  xhci-window-hi : Integer = 4294967296

  xhci-bar-usable : PciBar, Integer -> Boolean
  xhci-bar-usable (bar) (high) =
    if bar.bar-is-io then False
    else if high /= 0 then False
    else if bar.bar-base == 0 then False
    else if bar.bar-base >= xhci-window-hi then False
    else bar.bar-base >= xhci-window-lo

  xhci-reloc-base : Integer = #FE800000

 A machine with two xHCIs relocates twice, so the target cannot be one
 constant: the second controller would land on the first and every
 register pointer the first one handed out would address the second.
 The stride is 64 KB against a 16 KB register window, which keeps each
 controller on its own page and leaves room below the HD Audio BAR at
 #FE000000.

  xhci-reloc-stride : Integer = 65536

  xhci-reloc-base-for : Integer -> Integer
  xhci-reloc-base-for (ord) = xhci-reloc-base + ord * xhci-reloc-stride

  xhci-init-device : PciDevice, Integer -> [Device.Port] XhciHost
  xhci-init-device (d) (ord) = act
    en <- xhci-enable-device d
    bar <- pci-parse-bar (d.pci-bus) (d.pci-dev) (d.pci-func) 0
    hi <- pci-read-config (d.pci-bus) (d.pci-dev) (d.pci-func) 20
    let d0 = xdiag-put 1 1
    in let d1 = xdiag-put 2 (d.pci-vendor)
    in let d2 = xdiag-put 3 (d.pci-device-id)
    in let d3 = xdiag-put 0 xhci-diag-magic
    in let rt = xhci-intel-route d
    in let high = if bar.bar-is-64bit then hi else 0
    in let usable = xhci-bar-usable bar high
    in let vg = xdiag-put 30 (if usable then 1 else 2)
    in let vb = xdiag-put 31 (bar.bar-base)
    in let vh = xdiag-put 32 high
    in if usable then xhci-bring-up (bar.bar-base)
    else xhci-init-relocated d (bar.bar-is-64bit) ord
  end

  xhci-init-relocated : PciDevice, Boolean, Integer -> [Device.Port] XhciHost
  xhci-init-relocated (d) (is64) (ord) = act
    if is64 then xhci-init-relocated-64 d ord
    else xhci-init-relocated-32 d ord
  end

  xhci-init-relocated-64 : PciDevice, Integer -> [Device.Port] XhciHost
  xhci-init-relocated-64 (d) (ord) = act
    moved <- xhci-relocate-bar-64 d ord
    if moved == 0 then xhci-no-host
    else xhci-bring-up (xhci-reloc-base-for ord)
  end

  xhci-init-relocated-32 : PciDevice, Integer -> [Device.Port] XhciHost
  xhci-init-relocated-32 (d) (ord) = act
    moved <- xhci-relocate-bar-32 d ord
    if moved == 0 then xhci-no-host
    else xhci-bring-up (xhci-reloc-base-for ord)
  end

 Memory decoding goes off before the base moves and back on after, and
 nothing is trusted until the readback agrees. Only a 64-bit BAR owns
 the dword at offset 20: on a 32-bit BAR0 that offset is BAR1, another
 device register entirely, and zeroing it would relocate this controller
 by breaking something else.

  xhci-relocate-bar-64 : PciDevice, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  xhci-relocate-bar-64 (d) (ord) = act
    cmd <- pci-read-config (d.pci-bus) (d.pci-dev) (d.pci-func) 4
    w0 <- pci-write-config (d.pci-bus) (d.pci-dev) (d.pci-func) 4 (bit-and cmd (-3))
    w1 <- pci-write-config (d.pci-bus) (d.pci-dev) (d.pci-func) 16 (xhci-reloc-base-for ord)
    w2 <- pci-write-config (d.pci-bus) (d.pci-dev) (d.pci-func) 20 0
    w3 <- pci-write-config (d.pci-bus) (d.pci-dev) (d.pci-func) 4 (bit-or cmd 6)
    rb <- pci-read-config (d.pci-bus) (d.pci-dev) (d.pci-func) 16
    if bit-and rb 4294967280 == xhci-reloc-base-for ord then 1 else 0
  end

  xhci-relocate-bar-32 : PciDevice, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  xhci-relocate-bar-32 (d) (ord) = act
    cmd <- pci-read-config (d.pci-bus) (d.pci-dev) (d.pci-func) 4
    w0 <- pci-write-config (d.pci-bus) (d.pci-dev) (d.pci-func) 4 (bit-and cmd (-3))
    w1 <- pci-write-config (d.pci-bus) (d.pci-dev) (d.pci-func) 16 (xhci-reloc-base-for ord)
    w3 <- pci-write-config (d.pci-bus) (d.pci-dev) (d.pci-func) 4 (bit-or cmd 6)
    rb <- pci-read-config (d.pci-bus) (d.pci-dev) (d.pci-func) 16
    if bit-and rb 4294967280 == xhci-reloc-base-for ord then 1 else 0
  end

 Some controllers demand private scratchpad pages: HCSPARAMS2 states
 how many, and slot zero of the device context array must point at an
 array of that many page pointers before Run. A controller that asks
 and is not answered corrupts memory it was never given.

  xhci-scratch-count : Integer -> Integer
  xhci-scratch-count (hcs2) =
    bit-or (bit-shl (bit-and (bit-shru hcs2 21) 31) 5) (bit-and (bit-shru hcs2 27) 31)

  xhci-fill-scratch : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-fill-scratch (arr) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then 0
    else let page = alloc-zeroed 4096 4096
    in let lo = poke-32 arr (i * 8) page
    in let hi = poke-32 arr (i * 8 + 4) 0
    in xhci-fill-scratch arr (i + 1) n

  xhci-setup-scratch : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-setup-scratch (dcbaa) (count) =
    let arr = alloc-zeroed (count * 8 + 64) 64
    in let f = xhci-fill-scratch arr 0 count
    in let lo = poke-32 dcbaa 0 arr
    in poke-32 dcbaa 4 0

 Reset, program, run. The reset self-clears; CNR (controller not
 ready) must clear before any operational register is touched. The
 event ring segment table entry is written before ERSTBA, because
 the controller latches the segment address and size when ERSTBA
 lands.

  xhci-bring-up : Integer -> XhciHost
  xhci-bring-up (base) =
    let hb = xhci-reset-handback 0
    in let mm = xhci-map-mmio-hole
    in let caplen = bit-and (peek-32 base 0) 255
    in let op = base + caplen
    in let db = base + bit-and (peek-32 base xh-cap-dboff) (-4)
    in let rt = base + bit-and (peek-32 base xh-cap-rtsoff) (-32)
    in let hcs1 = peek-32 base xh-cap-hcsparams1
    in let hcc1 = peek-32 base xh-cap-hccparams1
    in let maxslots = bit-and hcs1 255
    in let maxports = bit-and (bit-shru hcs1 24) 255
    in let csz = bit-and (bit-shru hcc1 xh-hcc-csz-shift) 1
    in let ppc = bit-and (bit-shru hcc1 xh-hcc-ppc-shift) 1
    in let xecp = bit-and (bit-shru hcc1 16) 65535
    in let g4 = xdiag-put 4 caplen
    in let g5 = xdiag-put 5 hcc1
    in let g6 = xdiag-put 6 csz
    in let g7 = xdiag-put 7 ppc
    in let g8 = xdiag-put 8 xecp
    in let g19 = xdiag-put 19 op
    in let g13 = xdiag-put 13 maxslots
    in let g14 = xdiag-put 14 maxports
    in let own = xhci-take-ownership base xecp
    in let hlt = xhci-halt op
    in let r0 = poke-32 op xh-op-usbcmd xh-cmd-hcrst
    in let r1 = xhci-wait-bit-clear op xh-op-usbcmd xh-cmd-hcrst xhci-fuel
    in let r2 = xhci-wait-bit-clear op xh-op-usbsts xh-sts-cnr xhci-fuel
    in let g15 = xdiag-put 15 r1
    in let g16 = xdiag-put 16 r2
    in if r1 == 0 then xhci-no-host
    else if r2 == 0 then xhci-no-host
    else let dcbaa = alloc-zeroed 2048 64
    in let scount = xhci-scratch-count (peek-32 base xh-cap-hcsparams2)
    in let sp = if scount > 0 then xhci-setup-scratch dcbaa scount else 0
    in let cmd-ring = xhci-ring-new
    in let evt-ring = alloc-zeroed 1024 64
    in let erst = alloc-zeroed 64 64
    in let state = alloc-zeroed 1088 64
    in let c0 = poke-32 op xh-op-config maxslots
    in let c1 = poke-32 op xh-op-dcbaap dcbaa
    in let c2 = poke-32 op (xh-op-dcbaap + 4) 0
    in let c3 = poke-32 op xh-op-crcr (bit-or (xhci-ring-trbs cmd-ring) 1)
    in let c4 = poke-32 op (xh-op-crcr + 4) 0
    in let e0 = poke-32 erst 0 evt-ring
    in let e1 = poke-32 erst 4 0
    in let e2 = poke-32 erst 8 xhci-evt-count
    in let e3 = poke-32 rt xh-ir0-erstsz 1
    in let e4 = poke-32 rt xh-ir0-erstba erst
    in let e5 = poke-32 rt xh-ir0-erstba-hi 0
    in let e6 = poke-32 rt xh-ir0-erdp evt-ring
    in let e7 = poke-32 rt xh-ir0-erdp-hi 0
    in let s0 = poke-32 state xh-st-evt-cyc 1
    in let s1 = poke-32 state xh-st-tag 1
    in let g0 = poke-32 op xh-op-usbcmd xh-cmd-run
    in let g1 = xhci-wait-bit-clear op xh-op-usbsts xh-sts-hch xhci-fuel
    in let g17 = xdiag-put 17 g1
    in let pw = xhci-power-ports op 0 maxports
    in let dl = xhci-delay-ticks xhci-settle-ticks
    in let ps = xhci-diag-ports op 0 maxports 0
    in let gmg = xdiag-put 0 xhci-diag-magic
    in if g1 == 0 then xhci-no-host
    else XhciHost { xh-ok = True, xh-op = op, xh-db = db, xh-rt = rt, xh-ports = maxports, xh-dcbaa = dcbaa, xh-cmd-ring = cmd-ring, xh-evt-ring = evt-ring, xh-state = state, xh-ctxsz = if csz == 0 then 32 else 64, xh-cap = base, xh-xecp = xecp }

Section: Event Ring

 The controller produces events; the driver consumes them at its own
 dequeue index, accepting a TRB only when its cycle bit matches the
 driver's cycle state, flipping that state each time the index wraps.
 Each consumed event is acknowledged by writing ERDP back with the
 event-handler-busy bit, which is how a real controller learns the
 ring has room.

 xHCI delivers every endpoint of every device on the one event ring
 (one interrupter here), so an event consumed while waiting for a
 different (slot, endpoint) pair is latched by that pair. One byte of
 depth per pair is enough exactly because every ring in this driver
 keeps at most one TRB in flight.

  xhci-latch-at : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-latch-at (slot) (dci) =
    xh-st-latch + ((bit-and slot 31) * 32) + (bit-and dci 31)

  xhci-latch-put : XhciHost, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-latch-put (xh) (slot) (dci) (code) =
    poke-byte (xh.xh-state) (xhci-latch-at slot dci) code

  xhci-latch-take : XhciHost, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-latch-take (xh) (slot) (dci) =
    let v = peek-byte (xh.xh-state) (xhci-latch-at slot dci)
    in if v == 0 then 0
    else let c = poke-byte (xh.xh-state) (xhci-latch-at slot dci) 0
    in v

  xhci-evt-advance : XhciHost, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-evt-advance (xh) (deq) (cyc) =
    let st = xh.xh-state
    in let next = if deq + 1 >= xhci-evt-count then 0 else deq + 1
    in let w0 = poke-32 st xh-st-evt-deq next
    in let w1 = if next == 0 then poke-32 st xh-st-evt-cyc (1 - cyc) else 0
    in let a0 = poke-32 (xh.xh-rt) xh-ir0-erdp (bit-or (xh.xh-evt-ring + next * 16) 8)
    in poke-32 (xh.xh-rt) xh-ir0-erdp-hi 0

 The transfer wait returns the completion code as a bare integer --
 zero means no event -- because poll loops call it millions of times
 a second on a machine with no garbage collector, and a record return
 is a heap allocation per call. Its idle path is a latch peek, three
 more peeks, and a compare: nothing allocates.

  xhci-wait-xfer : XhciHost, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-wait-xfer (xh) (slot) (dci) (fuel) =
    let l = xhci-latch-take xh slot dci
    in if l /= 0 then l
    else let st = xh.xh-state
    in let deq = peek-32 st xh-st-evt-deq
    in let cyc = peek-32 st xh-st-evt-cyc
    in let ctl = peek-32 (xh.xh-evt-ring) (deq * 16 + 12)
    in if bit-and ctl 1 /= cyc then
      if fuel <= 0 then 0
      else xhci-wait-xfer xh slot dci (fuel - 1)
    else let sts = peek-32 (xh.xh-evt-ring) (deq * 16 + 8)
    in let adv = xhci-evt-advance xh deq cyc
    in let ty = bit-and (bit-shru ctl 10) 63
    in if ty /= trb-t-xfer-event then xhci-wait-xfer xh slot dci fuel
    else let code = bit-and (bit-shru sts 24) 255
    in let eslot = bit-and (bit-shru ctl 24) 255
    in let edci = bit-and (bit-shru ctl 16) 31
    in let mine = if eslot == slot then (if edci == dci then 1 else 0) else 0
    in if mine == 1 then code
    else let put = xhci-latch-put xh eslot edci code
    in xhci-wait-xfer xh slot dci fuel

  XhciEvent = record {
    xe-ok : Boolean,
    xe-code : Integer,
    xe-slot : Integer
  }

  xhci-no-event : XhciEvent
  xhci-no-event = XhciEvent { xe-ok = False, xe-code = 0, xe-slot = 0 }

  xhci-wait-event : XhciHost, Integer, Integer -> XhciEvent
  xhci-wait-event (xh) (want) (fuel) =
    let st = xh.xh-state
    in let deq = peek-32 st xh-st-evt-deq
    in let cyc = peek-32 st xh-st-evt-cyc
    in let ctl = peek-32 (xh.xh-evt-ring) (deq * 16 + 12)
    in if bit-and ctl 1 /= cyc then
      if fuel <= 0 then xhci-no-event
      else xhci-wait-event xh want (fuel - 1)
    else let sts = peek-32 (xh.xh-evt-ring) (deq * 16 + 8)
    in let adv = xhci-evt-advance xh deq cyc
    in let ty = bit-and (bit-shru ctl 10) 63
    in if ty == trb-t-xfer-event then
      let put = xhci-latch-put xh (bit-and (bit-shru ctl 24) 255) (bit-and (bit-shru ctl 16) 31) (bit-and (bit-shru sts 24) 255)
      in xhci-wait-event xh want fuel
    else if ty /= want then xhci-wait-event xh want fuel
    else XhciEvent { xe-ok = True, xe-code = bit-and (bit-shru sts 24) 255, xe-slot = bit-and (bit-shru ctl 24) 255 }

Section: Command Ring

 One command TRB, doorbell zero, then wait for the command completion
 event. Completion code 1 is success.

  xhci-command : XhciHost, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> XhciEvent
  xhci-command (xh) (plo) (phi) (sts) (ctl) =
    let w = xhci-ring-push (xh.xh-cmd-ring) plo phi sts ctl
    in let db = poke-32 (xh.xh-db) 0 0
    in xhci-wait-event xh trb-t-cmd-complete xhci-fuel

Section: Ports

 PORTSC is full of write-1-to-clear bits, so a port write must never
 echo the read value back; it writes only the intent (port power plus
 the operation) or the specific change bits being acknowledged. A
 USB3 device trains itself and shows up already enabled; a USB2
 device needs the reset, and enabled-ness appears when the reset
 completes.

  xhci-portsc : XhciHost, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-portsc (xh) (port) = peek-32 (xh.xh-op) (xh-op-ports + port * 16)

  xhci-find-port : XhciHost, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-find-port (xh) (from) =
    if from >= xh.xh-ports then -1
    else if bit-and (xhci-portsc xh from) xh-portsc-ccs /= 0 then from
    else xhci-find-port xh (from + 1)

  xhci-wait-ped : XhciHost, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-wait-ped (xh) (port) (fuel) =
    if bit-and (xhci-portsc xh port) xh-portsc-ped /= 0 then 1
    else if fuel <= 0 then 0
    else xhci-wait-ped xh port (fuel - 1)

  xhci-port-speed : XhciHost, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-port-speed (xh) (port) =
    xhci-speed-resolve (xh.xh-cap) (xh.xh-xecp) port (bit-and (bit-shru (xhci-portsc xh port) 10) 15)

  xhci-reset-port : XhciHost, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-reset-port (xh) (port) =
    let reg = xh-op-ports + port * 16
    in if bit-and (xhci-portsc xh port) xh-portsc-ped /= 0 then xhci-port-speed xh port
    else let r = poke-32 (xh.xh-op) reg (bit-or xh-portsc-pp xh-portsc-pr)
    in let ok = xhci-wait-ped xh port xhci-fuel
    in if ok == 0 then 0
    else let ack = poke-32 (xh.xh-op) reg (bit-or xh-portsc-pp (bit-or xh-portsc-csc xh-portsc-prc))
    in let rec = xhci-delay-ticks xhci-recovery-ticks
    in xhci-port-speed xh port

  xhci-ep0-maxpkt : Integer -> Integer
  xhci-ep0-maxpkt (speed) =
    if speed >= 4 then 512
    else if speed == 3 then 64
    else 8

Section: Input Contexts

 The controller learns about a device only through input contexts:
 Address Device reads the slot and endpoint-zero contexts, Configure
 Endpoint the slot and each added endpoint. Root-hub port numbers are
 one-based. Endpoint types in context encoding: control 4, bulk OUT
 2, bulk IN 6, interrupt IN 7. The dequeue pointer carries the ring's
 cycle state in bit zero; error-count three is the conventional
 bulk/control retry budget.

 A device behind a hub is named by the route string in slot-context
 dword zero, bits 19:0: one nibble per tier, each the one-based
 downstream port number of the hub at that tier, with the root port
 kept separately in dword one. A device on a root port has route
 zero. A low- or full-speed device below a HIGH-speed hub needs that
 hub's transaction translator: dword two names the parent hub's slot
 and the parent port, and the controller issues split transactions
 through them. Below a full-speed hub the whole chain runs at one
 speed and the fields stay zero.

 Endpoint-context dword zero carries the Interval field in bits
 23:16, the polling period as a power-of-two count of 125-microsecond
 microframes. Bulk endpoints ignore it; an interrupt endpoint with
 interval zero is a parameter error on a spec-strict controller. The
 encoding depends on device speed: full and low speed give bInterval
 in whole frames, so the exponent is the floor log of eight times
 that, clamped to the 3..10 window the spec demands; high and super
 speed give bInterval already as an exponent, one-based.

 Every context is ctxsz bytes -- 32 on a controller reporting CSZ=0
 (QEMU), 64 on most real Intel silicon (CSZ=1). The input control
 context sits at offset 0; the slot context at one context size; the
 endpoint contexts follow, endpoint-zero (device context index 1) at
 two context sizes. Writing the slot context at a hardcoded 32 on a
 64-byte controller lands it inside the input control context, and
 Address Device then reads a garbage slot -- a device that resets and
 connects but never addresses. A 64-byte input context with a high
 device-context index reaches 33 contexts, so the buffer is sized for
 that ceiling.

  xhci-ictx-address : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-ictx-address (route) (port) (speed) (maxpkt) (ep0-trbs) (ttslot) (ttport) (ctxsz) =
    let ictx = alloc-zeroed 2112 64
    in let sb = ctxsz
    in let eb = ctxsz * 2
    in let a0 = poke-32 ictx 4 3
    in let s0 = poke-32 ictx sb (bit-or route (bit-or (bit-shl 1 27) (bit-shl speed 20)))
    in let s1 = poke-32 ictx (sb + 4) (bit-shl (port + 1) 16)
    in let s2 = poke-32 ictx (sb + 8) (bit-or ttslot (bit-shl ttport 8))
    in let e0 = poke-32 ictx (eb + 4) (bit-or (bit-shl 4 3) (bit-or 6 (bit-shl maxpkt 16)))
    in let e1 = poke-32 ictx (eb + 8) (bit-or ep0-trbs 1)
    in let e2 = poke-32 ictx (eb + 12) 0
    in let e3 = poke-32 ictx (eb + 16) 8
    in ictx

  xhci-log2-floor : Integer -> Integer
  xhci-log2-floor (n) =
    if n <= 1 then 0
    else 1 + xhci-log2-floor (bit-shru n 1)

  xhci-ep-interval : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-ep-interval (speed) (binterval) =
    if speed >= 3 then
      let e = binterval - 1
      in if e < 0 then 0 else if e > 15 then 15 else e
    else let b = if binterval < 1 then 1 else binterval
    in let e = xhci-log2-floor (b * 8)
    in if e < 3 then 3 else if e > 10 then 10 else e

 The average-TRB-length dword doubles as the Max ESIT Payload for
 periodic endpoints: an interrupt endpoint moves at most one packet
 per service interval, so both halves are the packet size. Bulk keeps
 the conventional 512.

  xhci-ictx-ep : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-ictx-ep (ictx) (dci) (ep-type) (maxpkt) (trbs) (interval) (ctxsz) =
    let base = (dci + 1) * ctxsz
    in let d0 = poke-32 ictx base (bit-shl interval 16)
    in let d1 = poke-32 ictx (base + 4) (bit-or (bit-shl ep-type 3) (bit-or 6 (bit-shl maxpkt 16)))
    in let q0 = poke-32 ictx (base + 8) (bit-or trbs 1)
    in let q1 = poke-32 ictx (base + 12) 0
    in let avg = if ep-type == 7 then bit-or (bit-shl maxpkt 16) maxpkt else 512
    in poke-32 ictx (base + 16) avg

  xhci-ictx-configure : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-ictx-configure (dci-in) (pkt-in) (trbs-in) (dci-out) (pkt-out) (trbs-out) (ctxsz) (outctx) =
    let ictx = alloc-zeroed 2112 64
    in let maxdci = if dci-in > dci-out then dci-in else dci-out
    in let a0 = poke-32 ictx 4 (bit-or 1 (bit-or (bit-shl 1 dci-in) (bit-shl 1 dci-out)))
    in let cp = xhci-copy-ctx ictx outctx ctxsz 0
    in let s0 = poke-32 ictx ctxsz (bit-or (bit-and (peek-32 outctx 0) #07FFFFFF) (bit-shl maxdci 27))
    in let ei = xhci-ictx-ep ictx dci-in 6 pkt-in trbs-in 0 ctxsz
    in let eo = xhci-ictx-ep ictx dci-out 2 pkt-out trbs-out 0 ctxsz
    in ictx

 The single-endpoint variant, for a device whose interesting traffic
 is one interrupt endpoint: a HID keyboard's reports.

 The input slot context for a Configure Endpoint command is a copy
 of the current output slot context with only Context Entries
 changed (xHCI 4.6.3.2). Building a fresh one with Speed zero costs
 nothing for the asynchronous bulk endpoints -- their schedule does
 not depend on speed -- but a periodic (interrupt) endpoint is
 scheduled against the slot's speed, so a zero there leaves it
 unscheduled: one transfer at setup and then silence. Copy the whole
 slot context out of the device context (route, speed, root port,
 TT), then raise Context Entries to the highest device-context index
 in use.

  xhci-copy-ctx : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-copy-ctx (ictx) (outctx) (ctxsz) (i) =
    if i * 4 >= ctxsz then 0
    else let w = poke-32 ictx (ctxsz + i * 4) (peek-32 outctx (i * 4))
    in xhci-copy-ctx ictx outctx ctxsz (i + 1)

 Context Entries is the MAXIMUM device context index in use, so a second
 Configure Endpoint on a slot that already carries a higher endpoint must
 keep the higher figure: writing the new DCI unconditionally would drop
 an already-configured endpoint out of the slot (xHCI 6.2.2, Table 6-4).

  xhci-ictx-single : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-ictx-single (dci) (ep-type) (maxpkt) (trbs) (interval) (ctxsz) (outctx) =
    let ictx = alloc-zeroed 2112 64
    in let a0 = poke-32 ictx 4 (bit-or 1 (bit-shl 1 dci))
    in let cp = xhci-copy-ctx ictx outctx ctxsz 0
    in let cur = bit-shru (peek-32 outctx 0) 27
    in let ent = if cur > dci then cur else dci
    in let s0 = poke-32 ictx ctxsz (bit-or (bit-and (peek-32 outctx 0) #07FFFFFF) (bit-shl ent 27))
    in let e = xhci-ictx-ep ictx dci ep-type maxpkt trbs interval ctxsz
    in ictx

Section: Slot and Device

 Enable Slot returns the slot id in the completion event. The output
 device context handed to the controller through DCBAA is the
 controller's own memory from then on; the driver initializes the
 endpoint-zero dequeue there once for device models that read it
 directly (codex-vm) and never touches it again -- real controllers
 take ring positions only from input contexts.

 The output device context follows the same size, but with no input
 control context ahead of it: the slot context is at offset zero and
 endpoint context d at d context sizes.

  xhci-set-ep-ring : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-set-ep-ring (ctx) (dci) (trbs) (ctxsz) =
    let lo = poke-32 ctx (dci * ctxsz + 8) (bit-or trbs 1)
    in poke-32 ctx (dci * ctxsz + 12) 0

  xhci-open-device-at : XhciHost, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> UsbDev
  xhci-open-device-at (xh) (route) (rootport) (speed) (ttslot) (ttport) =
    let ev = xhci-command xh 0 0 0 (bit-shl trb-t-enable-slot 10)
    in if ev.xe-ok == False then xhci-no-dev
    else if ev.xe-code /= trb-cc-success then xhci-no-dev
    else let slot = ev.xe-slot
    in if slot == 0 then xhci-no-dev
    else let ctx = alloc-zeroed 2048 64
    in let ep0-ring = xhci-ring-new
    in let d0 = poke-32 (xh.xh-dcbaa) (slot * 8) ctx
    in let d1 = poke-32 (xh.xh-dcbaa) (slot * 8 + 4) 0
    in let e0 = xhci-set-ep-ring ctx 1 (xhci-ring-trbs ep0-ring) (xh.xh-ctxsz)
    in let ctxspeed = xhci-speed-encode (xh.xh-cap) (xh.xh-xecp) rootport speed
    in let ictx = xhci-ictx-address route rootport ctxspeed (xhci-ep0-maxpkt speed) (xhci-ring-trbs ep0-ring) ttslot ttport (xh.xh-ctxsz)
    in let av = xhci-command xh ictx 0 0 (bit-or (bit-shl trb-t-address-dev 10) (bit-shl slot 24))
    in if av.xe-ok == False then xhci-no-dev
    else if av.xe-code /= trb-cc-success then xhci-no-dev
    else UsbDev { ud-ok = True, ud-slot = slot, ud-port = rootport, ud-speed = speed, ud-ctx = ctx, ud-ep0-ring = ep0-ring, ud-route = route, ud-tt-slot = ttslot, ud-tt-port = ttport }

  xhci-open-device : XhciHost, Integer, Integer -> UsbDev
  xhci-open-device (xh) (port) (speed) = xhci-open-device-at xh 0 port speed 0 0

  xhci-configure-endpoint : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer -> XhciEvent
  xhci-configure-endpoint (xh) (dev) (ictx) =
    xhci-command xh ictx 0 0 (bit-or (bit-shl trb-t-config-ep 10) (bit-shl (dev.ud-slot) 24))

Section: Doorbells

  xhci-ring-doorbell : XhciHost, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-ring-doorbell (xh) (slot) (dci) = poke-32 (xh.xh-db) (slot * 4) dci

Section: Endpoint Recovery

 A device that refuses a command answers its bulk endpoint with STALL
 (completion code 6), and the endpoint stops there: the controller puts
 it in the Halted state and every later doorbell on it is ignored. Two
 halves have to be undone, and doing either alone leaves a pipe that
 looks recovered and moves nothing.

 The controller half is Reset Endpoint, which takes the endpoint out of
 Halted, followed by Set TR Dequeue Pointer -- mandatory after a reset,
 because the controller's saved position is the TRB that stalled and it
 will not resume past it on its own. The device half is CLEAR_FEATURE
 ENDPOINT_HALT on endpoint zero, which clears the condition and resets
 the data toggle at the far end; skip it and the device stalls again on
 the next packet.

 The endpoint address the device wants is not the device context index
 the controller wants: the DCI is the endpoint number doubled, plus one
 for an IN endpoint, while the wire address is the number with bit seven
 set for IN.

  xhci-reset-endpoint : XhciHost, Integer, Integer -> XhciEvent
  xhci-reset-endpoint (xh) (slot) (dci) =
    xhci-command xh 0 0 0 (bit-or (bit-shl trb-t-reset-ep 10) (bit-or (bit-shl dci 16) (bit-shl slot 24)))

 Stop Endpoint is the one command that makes the controller publish its
 true position: xHCI 4.6.9 requires it to write the internal dequeue
 pointer back into the output endpoint context and park the state at
 Stopped. Ringing the doorbell afterwards resumes from exactly there --
 no Set TR Dequeue needed unless the position is to CHANGE.

  xhci-stop-endpoint : XhciHost, Integer, Integer -> XhciEvent
  xhci-stop-endpoint (xh) (slot) (dci) =
    xhci-command xh 0 0 0 (bit-or (bit-shl trb-t-stop-ep 10) (bit-or (bit-shl dci 16) (bit-shl slot 24)))

  xhci-set-tr-dequeue : XhciHost, Integer, Integer, Integer -> XhciEvent
  xhci-set-tr-dequeue (xh) (slot) (dci) (ring) =
    xhci-command xh (bit-or (xhci-ring-enq-addr ring) (xhci-ring-cycle ring)) 0 0 (bit-or (bit-shl trb-t-set-deq 10) (bit-or (bit-shl dci 16) (bit-shl slot 24)))

  xhci-dci-to-ep-addr : Integer -> Integer
  xhci-dci-to-ep-addr (dci) =
    if bit-and dci 1 == 1 then bit-or (bit-shru (dci - 1) 1) 128
    else bit-shru dci 1

  usb-clear-halt : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer -> Boolean
  usb-clear-halt (xh) (dev) (ep-addr) =
    xhci-ctrl-nodata xh dev 2 1 0 ep-addr

 Both halves, in the order the spec requires: the controller is taken
 out of Halted and repositioned first, so that the control transfer
 clearing the device half runs on a controller that can carry the
 endpoint again afterwards.

  xhci-recover-endpoint : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  xhci-recover-endpoint (xh) (dev) (dci) (ring) =
    let r = xhci-reset-endpoint xh (dev.ud-slot) dci
    in if r.xe-ok == False then False
    else if r.xe-code /= trb-cc-success then False
    else let q = xhci-set-tr-dequeue xh (dev.ud-slot) dci ring
    in if q.xe-ok == False then False
    else if q.xe-code /= trb-cc-success then False
    else usb-clear-halt xh dev (xhci-dci-to-ep-addr dci)

 The default control pipe recovers with the HOST half only, and the
 function above must not be used for it. Its last step is
 CLEAR_FEATURE ENDPOINT_HALT, which is a control transfer -- on endpoint
 zero that request would have to travel through the very pipe it is
 recovering. USB 2.0 8.5.3.4 also makes it unnecessary: a control
 endpoint's halt is cleared by the next SETUP, which a device is
 required to accept even while halted, so there is no device half to
 clear.

  xhci-recover-ep0 : XhciHost, UsbDev -> Boolean
  xhci-recover-ep0 (xh) (dev) =
    let r = xhci-reset-endpoint xh (dev.ud-slot) 1
    in if r.xe-ok == False then False
    else if r.xe-code /= trb-cc-success then False
    else let q = xhci-set-tr-dequeue xh (dev.ud-slot) 1 (dev.ud-ep0-ring)
    in if q.xe-ok == False then False
    else q.xe-code == trb-cc-success

Section: Control Transfers

 A control transfer is three TRBs on the endpoint-zero ring: SETUP
 with the eight setup bytes immediate in the parameter dwords, DATA
 pointing at the buffer, STATUS closing the handshake in the opposite
 direction of the data. Only the status TRB carries IOC, so exactly
 one transfer event answers the whole transfer; a short data stage is
 normal (a descriptor read asks for more than most descriptors hold)
 and ends the data TD silently.

  xhci-setup-lo : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-setup-lo (rt) (req) (value) =
    bit-or rt (bit-or (bit-shl req 8) (bit-shl (bit-and value 65535) 16))

  xhci-setup-hi : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-setup-hi (index) (length) =
    bit-or (bit-and index 65535) (bit-shl (bit-and length 65535) 16)

  xhci-ctrl-in : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  xhci-ctrl-in (xh) (dev) (rt) (req) (value) (index) (buf) (len) =
    let ring = dev.ud-ep0-ring
    in let w0 = xhci-ring-push ring (xhci-setup-lo rt req value) (xhci-setup-hi index len) 8 (bit-or (bit-shl trb-t-setup 10) (bit-or trb-flag-idt (bit-shl 3 16)))
    in let w1 = xhci-ring-push ring buf 0 len (bit-or (bit-shl trb-t-data 10) (bit-shl 1 16))
    in let w2 = xhci-ring-push ring 0 0 0 (bit-or (bit-shl trb-t-status 10) trb-flag-ioc)
    in let db = xhci-ring-doorbell xh (dev.ud-slot) 1
    in let code = xhci-wait-xfer xh (dev.ud-slot) 1 xhci-fuel
    in if code == trb-cc-success then True
    else code == trb-cc-short

 The completion code is the diagnosis and the Boolean throws it away. A
 STALL is the device refusing a request it understood, a Transaction Error
 is the wire, and the fuel running out is neither -- three different next
 steps rendered as one `False`. Callers that only branch keep the Boolean;
 a caller standing at a failure it has to explain takes the code.

  xhci-ctrl-nodata-code : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  xhci-ctrl-nodata-code (xh) (dev) (rt) (req) (value) (index) =
    let ring = dev.ud-ep0-ring
    in let w0 = xhci-ring-push ring (xhci-setup-lo rt req value) (xhci-setup-hi index 0) 8 (bit-or (bit-shl trb-t-setup 10) trb-flag-idt)
    in let w1 = xhci-ring-push ring 0 0 0 (bit-or (bit-shl trb-t-status 10) (bit-or trb-flag-ioc (bit-shl 1 16)))
    in let db = xhci-ring-doorbell xh (dev.ud-slot) 1
    in xhci-wait-xfer xh (dev.ud-slot) 1 xhci-fuel

  xhci-ctrl-nodata : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  xhci-ctrl-nodata (xh) (dev) (rt) (req) (value) (index) =
    xhci-ctrl-nodata-code xh dev rt req value index == trb-cc-success

Section: Standard Requests

  usb-get-descriptor : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  usb-get-descriptor (xh) (dev) (desc-type) (desc-index) (buf) (len) =
    xhci-ctrl-in xh dev 128 6 (bit-or (bit-shl desc-type 8) desc-index) 0 buf len

  usb-set-configuration-code : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer -> Integer
  usb-set-configuration-code (xh) (dev) (config) =
    xhci-ctrl-nodata-code xh dev 0 9 config 0

  usb-set-configuration : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer -> Boolean
  usb-set-configuration (xh) (dev) (config) =
    usb-set-configuration-code xh dev config == trb-cc-success
